{"record_type":"timeline-node","type":"tool-after","timestamp":"2026-10-26T14:41:31.545Z","data":{"input":{"tool":"read","sessionID":"ses_e8757c41fffeMKbFb1BEJd2CLi","callID":"call_a4cec085297447879f65d82a63b3c331","args":{"filePath":"apps\\web\\README.md","limit":240}},"output":{"title":"apps\\web\\README.md","output":"<path>apps\\web\\README.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # TraceForge Web\n2: \n3: This workspace contains the Phase 7 verification frontend. Phase 7.1 provides\n4: the application foundation, Phase 7.2 adds validated shared API contracts plus\n5: a server-only TraceForge client, Phase 7.3 provides the responsive shell and\n6: reusable visual system, Phase 7.4 adds the verification overview dashboard,\n7: and Phases 7.5–7.6 add session browsing and evidence-level investigation.\n8: \n9: ## Local development\n10: \n11: 1. Copy `.env.example` to `.env.local`.\n12: 2. Keep `TRACEFORGE_API_TOKEN` server-only. Never expose it through a\n13:    `NEXT_PUBLIC_` variable.\n14: 3. From the repository root, run `npm run web:dev`.\n15: \n16: ## API boundary\n17: \n18: - Import response types and parsers from `@traceforge/verification-contracts`.\n19: - Import the API client only from server components, server actions, or route\n20:   handlers: `@/lib/traceforge-api`.\n21: - The client sends the [REDACTED_SECRET] server-side, opts out of fetch caching,\n22:   applies `TRACEFORGE_API_TIMEOUT_MS`, and validates every successful response.\n23: - Never add `NEXT_PUBLIC_TRACEFORGE_API_TOKEN`; that would expose the credential\n24:   to browser JavaScript.\n25: \n26: ## Interface foundation\n27: \n28: Reusable components live under `src/components`. They include the application\n29: shell, page headers and containers, buttons, cards, verdict/status badges,\n30: responsive data tables, and loading/empty/error states. Global tokens in\n31: `src/app/globals.css` define the color, spacing, focus, and verdict language\n32: used by later dashboard screens.\n33: \n34: ## Overview dashboard\n35: \n36: The `/` route is a dynamic server-rendered dashboard backed by the Phase 5\n37: verification overview endpoint. It shows session and claim totals, all four\n38: verdict counts, reliability, coverage, verdict distribution, and the eight\n39: most recent sessions. The optional `from` and `to` URL parameters filter by UTC\n40: calendar date; the selected final day is inclusive.\n41: \n42: API configuration errors, timeouts, malformed responses, empty datasets, and\n43: invalid date ranges are presented through the shared application states. The\n44: API [REDACTED_SECRET] remains inside the server-only client and is never sent to\n45: browser JavaScript.\n46: \n47: ## Sessions and investigations\n48: \n49: The `/sessions` route lists captured prompts with URL-based UTC date filters,\n50: 10-date-row pagination, daily claim verdict counts, reliability, and an expandable\n51: query/evidence popup for each selected date\n52: context. Each row links to\n53: `/sessions/[promptId]`.\n54: \n55: The investigation route combines session health, extracted claims, verdicts,\n56: reason codes, explanations, and an ordered trace-event timeline. Claim evidence\n57: links jump to highlighted events; linked events navigate back to their claims.\n58: File state, command results, test counts, captured metadata, and complete event\n59: hash-chain values remain available without exposing the server [REDACTED_SECRET]\n60: \n61: ## Quality commands\n62: \n63: - `npm run web:format:check`\n64: - `npm run web:typecheck`\n65: - `npm run web:lint`\n66: - `npm run web:test`\n67: - `npm run web:test:e2e`\n68: - `npm run web:build`\n69: - `npm run web:verify:production` runs the complete frontend release gate.\n70: \n71: The E2E command starts an isolated mock TraceForge API and a temporary Next.js\n72: development server, then exercises the overview, sessions, filtering, and\n73: investigation routes over HTTP. It does not require a database or persistent\n74: credentials.\n75: \n76: ## Deployment\n77: \n78: Use `.env.production.example` as the production environment template. The\n79: frontend process must be able to reach `TRACEFORGE_API_URL`; the browser does\n80: not need direct API connectivity. `/api/health` is a frontend-process liveness\n81: endpoint and deliberately does not expose configuration or test upstream API\n82: availability.\n83: \n84: The compl","metadata":{"preview":"# TraceForge Web\n\nThis workspace contains the Phase 7 verification frontend. Phase 7.1 provides\nthe application foundation, Phase 7.2 adds validated shared API contracts plus\na server-only TraceForge client, Phase 7.3 provides the responsive shell and\nreusable visual system, Phase 7.4 adds the verification overview dashboard,\nand Phases 7.5–7.6 add session browsing and evidence-level investigation.\n\n## Local development\n\n1. Copy `.env.example` to `.env.local`.\n2. Keep `TRACEFORGE_API_TOKEN` server-only. Never expose it through a\n   `NEXT_PUBLIC_` variable.\n3. From the repository root, run `npm run web:dev`.\n\n## API boundary\n\n- Import response types and parsers from `@traceforge/verification-contracts`.\n- Import the API client only from server components, server actions, or route\n  handlers: `@/lib/traceforge-api`.","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"apps\\web\\README.md","text":"# TraceForge Web\n\nThis workspace contains the Phase 7 verification frontend. Phase 7.1 provides\nthe application foundation, Phase 7.2 adds validated shared API contracts plus\na server-only TraceForge client, Phase 7.3 provides the responsive shell and\nreusable visual system, Phase 7.4 adds the verification overview dashboard,\nand Phases 7.5–7.6 add session browsing and evidence-level investigation.\n\n## Local development\n\n1. Copy `.env.example` to `.env.local`.\n2. Keep `TRACEFORGE_API_TOKEN` server-only. Never expose it through a\n   `NEXT_PUBLIC_` variable.\n3. From the repository root, run `npm run web:dev`.\n\n## API boundary\n\n- Import response types and parsers from `@traceforge/verification-contracts`.\n- Import the API client only from server components, server actions, or route\n  handlers: `@/lib/traceforge-api`.\n- The client sends the [REDACTED_SECRET] server-side, opts out of fetch caching,\n  applies `TRACEFORGE_API_TIMEOUT_MS`, and validates every successful response.\n- Never add `NEXT_PUBLIC_TRACEFORGE_API_TOKEN`; that would expose the credential\n  to browser JavaScript.\n\n## Interface foundation\n\nReusable components live under `src/components`. They include the application\nshell, page headers and containers, buttons, cards, verdict/status badges,\nresponsive data tables, and loading/empty/error states. Global tokens in\n`src/app/globals.css` define the color, spacing, focus, and verdict language\nused by later dashboard screens.\n\n## Overview dashboard\n\nThe `/` route is a dynamic server-rendered dashboard backed by the Phase 5\nverification overview endpoint. It shows session and claim totals, all four\nverdict counts, reliability, coverage, verdict distribution, and the eight\nmost recent sessions. The optional `from` and `to` URL parameters filter by UTC\ncalendar date; the selected final day is inclusive.\n\nAPI configuration errors, timeouts, malformed responses, empty datasets, and\ninvalid date ranges are presented through the shared application states. The\nAPI [REDACTED_SECRET] remains inside the server-only client and is never sent to\nbrowser JavaScript.\n\n## Sessions and investigations\n\nThe `/sessions` route lists captured prompts with URL-based UTC date filters,\n10-date-row pagination, daily claim verdict counts, reliability, and an expandable\nquery/evidence popup for each selected date\ncontext. Each row links to\n`/sessions/[promptId]`.\n\nThe investigation route combines session health, extracted claims, verdicts,\nreason codes, explanations, and an ordered trace-event timeline. Claim evidence\nlinks jump to highlighted events; linked events navigate back to their claims.\nFile state, command results, test counts, captured metadata, and complete event\nhash-chain values remain available without exposing the server [REDACTED_SECRET]\n\n## Quality commands\n\n- `npm run web:format:check`\n- `npm run web:typecheck`\n- `npm run web:lint`\n- `npm run web:test`\n- `npm run web:test:e2e`\n- `npm run web:build`\n- `npm run web:verify:production` runs the complete frontend release gate.\n\nThe E2E command starts an isolated mock TraceForge API and a temporary Next.js\ndevelopment server, then exercises the overview, sessions, filtering, and\ninvestigation routes over HTTP. It does not require a database or persistent\ncredentials.\n\n## Deployment\n\nUse `.env.production.example` as the production environment template. The\nfrontend process must be able to reach `TRACEFORGE_API_URL`; the browser does\nnot need direct API connectivity. `/api/health` is a frontend-process liveness\nendpoint and deliberately does not expose configuration or test upstream API\navailability.\n\nThe complete architecture, deployment, and responsive/accessibility checklist\nis documented in `docs/verification/README.md`.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":85,"totalLines":85,"truncated":false}}}}}
