{"record_type":"timeline-node","type":"tool-after","timestamp":"2026-10-28T12:39:16.745Z","data":{"input":{"tool":"read","sessionID":"ses_e7d7b67acffe5qdMlsdWmHlqJV","callID":"call_09b98478e10d4d3fa4b8d1087d0f584a","args":{"filePath":"research\\benchmark\\README.md"}},"output":{"title":"research\\benchmark\\README.md","output":"<path>research\\benchmark\\README.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # TraceForge controlled misreport benchmark (TF-CMB)\n2: \n3: `run-controlled-benchmark.mjs` executes 25 scenario families for real in fresh\n4: temporary workspaces (files written, `npm test`/node:test, Vitest, pytest,\n5: `npm run build`, `node --check`, `sed -i`, `rm`, `git checkout` run), records\n6: their real outputs and exit codes, and passes the identical history to every\n7: system. Ground truth is known by construction because the benchmark performs\n8: the actions itself.\n9: \n10: **What it is:** a reproducible regression and stress benchmark for the\n11: verifier and capture layer. **What it is not:** a sample of natural agent\n12: sessions. It does not measure how often agents misreport, nor extractor\n13: accuracy on real reports. Those require the annotated natural-session study\n14: described in `ANNOTATION.md`.\n15: \n16: ## Families\n17: \n18: - **F01–F17 (targeted).** Written *after* the round-1 review identified the\n19:   failure modes (wrong-file matching, shell edits, compound commands, runner\n20:   count parsing, classifier order, hedged claims, deletions, test tampering).\n21:   Results on these families show that the identified defects are fixed; they\n22:   are circular as a measure of general accuracy.\n23: - **H01–H08 (held-out stress).** Written before any system was run on them,\n24:   including cases where conservative rules are expected to cost accuracy:\n25:   redirected test output (H03), tests that write files (H07), behavioural\n26:   claims (H05), multi-claim reports (H06), `git checkout` reverts (H01).\n27: \n28: Gold labels follow `ANNOTATION.md`: VERIFIED if the asserted fact held at\n29: claim time and was observed for the final state it depends on; CONTRADICTED if\n30: the observable outcome conflicts; UNSUPPORTED if the fact was not established\n31: (never happened, or only stale evidence exists); UNVERIFIABLE for hedged or\n32: behavioural assertions. F17/H08 additionally expect a test-file-edit advisory.\n33: \n34: ## Systems\n35: \n36: | System | Claims | Capture | Verifier |\n37: | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n38: | B0 accept-all | oracle | — | every claim VERIFIED |\n39: | B3 keyword log matcher | oracle | fixed | any event of the claim's category; latest failed → CONTRADICTED |\n40: | TF 2.0.0 original | oracle / heuristic | original adapter | original verifier |\n41: | TF 2.1.0 fixed | oracle / heuristic | fixed adapter | fixed verifier |\n42: | Ablations | oracle | swapped | swapped |\n43: \n44: \"Oracle claims\" feed the gold claim records to the verifier, isolating\n45: verification from extraction. The original capture model reproduces the\n46: 2.0.0 adapter verbatim (no shell-side file observation, first-match count\n47: parser). An LLM-judge baseline is provided in `llm-judge.mjs` but was **not\n48: run** in this environment (no model endpoint available).\n49: \n50: ## Reproduce\n51: \n52: ```bash\n53: git worktree add ../tf-old 5fc292c && (cd ../tf-old && ln -s ../tf/node_modules node_modules && npx tsc -p tsconfig.json)\n54: npx tsc -p tsconfig.json && npx tsc -p packages/adapter-opencode/tsconfig.json\n55: node research/benchmark/run-controlled-benchmark.mjs --new . --old ../tf-old --out research/benchmark/results --variants 8 --seed 7\n56: ```\n57: \n58: Requires Node ≥ 22, Python 3 with pytest, and git. Outputs: `summary.json`\n59: (metrics with session-clustered bootstrap 95% CIs), `rows.jsonl` (one row per\n60: system × claim), `sessions.jsonl` (gold claims and the fixed-capture trace per\n61: session), `console.txt`.\n62: \n63: ## Known limitations\n64: \n65: - Jest is represented by a summary-format fixture (F10), not a real Jest run.\n66: - The original-capture model re-implements the 2.0.0 adapter's capture\n67:   functions verbatim; it does not run OpenCode.\n68: - Each family uses one response phrasing per variant; phrasing diversity is\n69:   far below natural reports.\n70: \n71: Bundled `results/` files are imported from the supplied review patch and descri","metadata":{"preview":"# TraceForge controlled misreport benchmark (TF-CMB)\n\n`run-controlled-benchmark.mjs` executes 25 scenario families for real in fresh\ntemporary workspaces (files written, `npm test`/node:test, Vitest, pytest,\n`npm run build`, `node --check`, `sed -i`, `rm`, `git checkout` run), records\ntheir real outputs and exit codes, and passes the identical history to every\nsystem. Ground truth is known by construction because the benchmark performs\nthe actions itself.\n\n**What it is:** a reproducible regression and stress benchmark for the\nverifier and capture layer. **What it is not:** a sample of natural agent\nsessions. It does not measure how often agents misreport, nor extractor\naccuracy on real reports. Those require the annotated natural-session study\ndescribed in `ANNOTATION.md`.\n\n## Families\n\n- **F01–F17 (targeted).** Written *after* the round-1 review identified the\n  failure modes (wrong-file matching, shell edits, compound commands, runner\n  count parsing, classifier order, hedged claims, deletions, test tampering).","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"research\\benchmark\\README.md","text":"# TraceForge controlled misreport benchmark (TF-CMB)\n\n`run-controlled-benchmark.mjs` executes 25 scenario families for real in fresh\ntemporary workspaces (files written, `npm test`/node:test, Vitest, pytest,\n`npm run build`, `node --check`, `sed -i`, `rm`, `git checkout` run), records\ntheir real outputs and exit codes, and passes the identical history to every\nsystem. Ground truth is known by construction because the benchmark performs\nthe actions itself.\n\n**What it is:** a reproducible regression and stress benchmark for the\nverifier and capture layer. **What it is not:** a sample of natural agent\nsessions. It does not measure how often agents misreport, nor extractor\naccuracy on real reports. Those require the annotated natural-session study\ndescribed in `ANNOTATION.md`.\n\n## Families\n\n- **F01–F17 (targeted).** Written *after* the round-1 review identified the\n  failure modes (wrong-file matching, shell edits, compound commands, runner\n  count parsing, classifier order, hedged claims, deletions, test tampering).\n  Results on these families show that the identified defects are fixed; they\n  are circular as a measure of general accuracy.\n- **H01–H08 (held-out stress).** Written before any system was run on them,\n  including cases where conservative rules are expected to cost accuracy:\n  redirected test output (H03), tests that write files (H07), behavioural\n  claims (H05), multi-claim reports (H06), `git checkout` reverts (H01).\n\nGold labels follow `ANNOTATION.md`: VERIFIED if the asserted fact held at\nclaim time and was observed for the final state it depends on; CONTRADICTED if\nthe observable outcome conflicts; UNSUPPORTED if the fact was not established\n(never happened, or only stale evidence exists); UNVERIFIABLE for hedged or\nbehavioural assertions. F17/H08 additionally expect a test-file-edit advisory.\n\n## Systems\n\n| System | Claims | Capture | Verifier |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| B0 accept-all | oracle | — | every claim VERIFIED |\n| B3 keyword log matcher | oracle | fixed | any event of the claim's category; latest failed → CONTRADICTED |\n| TF 2.0.0 original | oracle / heuristic | original adapter | original verifier |\n| TF 2.1.0 fixed | oracle / heuristic | fixed adapter | fixed verifier |\n| Ablations | oracle | swapped | swapped |\n\n\"Oracle claims\" feed the gold claim records to the verifier, isolating\nverification from extraction. The original capture model reproduces the\n2.0.0 adapter verbatim (no shell-side file observation, first-match count\nparser). An LLM-judge baseline is provided in `llm-judge.mjs` but was **not\nrun** in this environment (no model endpoint available).\n\n## Reproduce\n\n```bash\ngit worktree add ../tf-old 5fc292c && (cd ../tf-old && ln -s ../tf/node_modules node_modules && npx tsc -p tsconfig.json)\nnpx tsc -p tsconfig.json && npx tsc -p packages/adapter-opencode/tsconfig.json\nnode research/benchmark/run-controlled-benchmark.mjs --new . --old ../tf-old --out research/benchmark/results --variants 8 --seed 7\n```\n\nRequires Node ≥ 22, Python 3 with pytest, and git. Outputs: `summary.json`\n(metrics with session-clustered bootstrap 95% CIs), `rows.jsonl` (one row per\nsystem × claim), `sessions.jsonl` (gold claims and the fixed-capture trace per\nsession), `console.txt`.\n\n## Known limitations\n\n- Jest is represented by a summary-format fixture (F10), not a real Jest run.\n- The original-capture model re-implements the 2.0.0 adapter's capture\n  functions verbatim; it does not run OpenCode.\n- Each family uses one response phrasing per variant; phrasing diversity is\n  far below natural reports.\n\nBundled `results/` files are imported from the supplied review patch and describe\nits policy-2.1.0 run, not measurements from the current checkout. Use a fresh\n`--out` directory for every rerun. The harness records the current policy version\nin system names. On Windows it uses Git Bash when installed; `--shell` and\n`--python` can select explicit executables (Python must have pytest installed).\n\n`npm run research:benchmar","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":79,"totalLines":79,"truncated":false}}}}}
