{"record_type":"timeline-node","type":"event","timestamp":"2026-10-28T12:44:02.957Z","data":{"event":{"id":"evt_18289fa08001SpPDEpo264od72","type":"message.part.updated","properties":{"sessionID":"ses_e7d7ba936ffeOnBhTlKsfisQhw","part":{"id":"prt_1828998a4001Y28wYs2CElsAY5","messageID":"msg_18289914c001aHb50rxFzJt2DI","sessionID":"ses_e7d7ba936ffeOnBhTlKsfisQhw","type":"reasoning","text":"Evidence is rich. We need decide evidence references for worksheets. Include eventId, seq, type, outcome, target, occurredAt, metadata summary. Specifically to hide predicted claims/verdicts, omit claims.csv and goldClaims. Evidence itself may include \"target\" and metadata only, no predictions. Fine.\n\nNeed inspect dataset for evidence references and maybe some anomalous sessions with repeated final response captured and incomplete captures. There's capture completeness field on adjudication. We need use it.\n\nPlan:\n1. Add script(s) under research/annotation/ or research/datasets/tools:\n   - `research/annotation/prepare-worksheets.mjs`: reads natural-v1.jsonl, writes `natural-v1.annotator-a.csv`, `natural-v1.annotator-b.csv`, and `natural-v1.adjudication-template.csv` maybe plus README/instructions. But user only asks two worksheets. We can add this generator and run it. It must use only id/prompt/finalResponse/evidence, exclude goldClaims and predicted claims. Include a `blind` marker. Support claim slots? Let's define one row per session times `MAX_CLAIMS` maybe 8. But evidence references repeating. Or one row per session with a \"claim_annotations_json\" column where annotator writes JSON array? That's less human-friendly. Better two files: per-session context CSV and claim-level JSON? Hmm.\n\nMaybe directly create per-annotator `.csv` with one row per session and columns:\nsession_id, prompt, final_response, evidence_references, claim_id, source_quote, claim_type, target, expected_result, verdict, rationale, evidence_event_ids, test_tampering, capture_completeness, annotator_notes\n\nInstruct: duplicate a session's row once per claim, keeping session_id/context identical. That's simple and no bloat. Conversion can aggregate by session and claim_id. But duplicate rows with long prompt/evidence is annoying. Could use `claim_slot` preallocated 1-12 with blank claims. Maybe not.\n\nActually annotators likely use spreadsheet with one row per claim; repeated context can be handy. The existing natural-v1.annotations.csv is one row per session, so they'd insert rows. Given user wants worksheets \"include session ID, prompt, final response, and evidence references\", one row per session satisfies. Add clear \"One row per claim; copy the session context row as needed.\" But if no claims, annotators leave blank. Need distinguish unannotated blank from \"no claims\". Add `review_status` required (COMPLETE / NO_CLAIMS / INCOMPLETE) and `capture_completeness`.\n\nMaybe pre-populate 5 claim slots per session in separate worksheet? That ensures direct fill. But 40*10 rows. Let's assess size: natural jsonl 221KB includes evidence; worksheets 2*40*? If 10 slots, maybe 5MB each. Acceptable but noisy. CSV fields with newlines and quotes. Could be okay.\n\nI think better create:\n- Two annotation worksheets as JSONL? Each line includes full prompt/final/evidence refs plus a `claims` array template (empty) to fill. JSONL is harder for humans but can be opened in editors; not spreadsheet.\n- User explicitly calls them worksheets likely CSV. Use CSV.\n\nLet's create two CSVs with one row per session × 6 claim slots? Most sessions have ~6.5 claims, max maybe 20. But we can use claim_slot up to 25? Too big. One row per session and instructions to duplicate is cleaner.\n\nAlternative two files per annotator: `...annotator-a.csv` (session context) and `...annotator-a-claims.csv` (blank claim rows referencing session). But \"two separate annotation worksheets\" still can be two CSVs per annotator? No, two separate worksheets total. Could bundle context and blank claim rows in one CSV.\n\nLet's use one row per session and claim fields as semicolon? No.\n\nMaybe create CSV with one row per session and columns `claims_json` where they fill an array; conversion can parse. But source quote etc in JSON is prone to errors.\n\nCould create one row per session per claim slot but only 4 slots; annotators can add rows. Evidence refs repeated. We can cap 6 slots and say add rows if more than 6. Hm","time":{"start":1793191418020,"end":1793191442952}},"time":1793191442952}}}}
