{"record_type":"timeline-node","type":"event","timestamp":"2026-10-28T13:04:41.299Z","data":{"event":{"id":"evt_1829cdf51001IHdKp14TkSwN22","type":"message.part.updated","properties":{"sessionID":"ses_e7d7ba936ffeOnBhTlKsfisQhw","part":{"id":"prt_1829cbd87001oi21S3HwXY20ap","messageID":"msg_1829cb22b00150C8mVxUevmns1","sessionID":"ses_e7d7ba936ffeOnBhTlKsfisQhw","type":"reasoning","text":"The benchmark uses substring matching, not exact. The evaluate harness uses exact/relaxed. To fairly evaluate heuristic, either use relaxed matching (token F1 >= 0.8) or normalize gold quotes. The previous bundled benchmark's heuristic accuracy was based on substring matching. For our controlled evaluation, we should use relaxed matching for extraction alignment or normalize. The user wants best evaluation. The paper might use strict exact? README says run both exact and relaxed separately. We can run exact and relaxed, and report both. But heuristic exact 0 due period mismatch is a known span-normalization artifact; relaxed should be fair. We can run controlled suite with `--matching relaxed` for extraction. But judge baselines with oracle also unaffected? Exact vs relaxed changes alignment; oracle exact matches. For consistency, we can run both exact and relaxed and report. The runner can run matching exact only; we can run a second suite with relaxed. That doubles fast, but model runs would double too. We don't want double model calls for Ollama/judges. Could run fast exact+relaxed, and model runs only relaxed (or exact). For fair extraction, relaxed. For oracle/judge, exact fine. But paper wants consistency. Hmm.\n\nMaybe better fix controlled dataset gold quotes to full sentence including punctuation. Then exact matches heuristic. This is preferable for a clean controlled dataset. The controlled labels are derived by construction anyway; we can make the quote span exact full sentence. We must preserve label semantics. For every gold quote, extend to the sentence containing it. Since all responses are short and claims are sentences, we can simply use the entire final response as sourceQuote for single-claim sessions. For multi-claim H06, split by `. ` into sentences. This would yield exact spans matching heuristic. But then for H06, three claims all in one response; exact quotes separated by sentence. Fine.\n\nCould implement a function `sentenceSpan(response, quote)`:\n- Find index of quote.\n- If quote already ends with terminal punctuation and next chars are boundary/end, return quote.\n- Else expand start to previous sentence boundary? The quote starts at sentence start in all cases. Expand end to next terminal `.`, `!`, `?` followed by whitespace/end. Include punctuation.\n- For F16 hedged, quote is sentence without period; expand to period.\n- For H06, quote \"Created `src/a.ts`\" followed by \".\" -> expand to \"Created `src/a.ts`.\" Good.\n- For H02 \"Ran the unit tests successfully\" -> \"Ran ... successfully.\" Good.\nThis will make exact match. But the gold claim text in benchmark uses no period; we are adding punctuation. Is that acceptable? It's an exact sentence span from the response, semantically same. We can document \"controlled label quotes normalized to the full sentence so exact alignment is not penalized by terminal punctuation.\" That's transparent.\n\nBut for the paper's controlled benchmark results, the existing summary uses no period. Our new controlled evaluation uses full sentence. Fine, separate.\n\nAlternatively use relaxed matching and keep labels. The exact alignment issue is not a bug in our tooling but a dataset-format mismatch. The user wants fix all. We can fix dataset to full sentence. Let's do that. Then rerun fast eval and expect heuristic matches. Need ensure all gold quotes can be expanded exactly. Let's write function in converter:\n```\nfunction sentenceQuote(response, quote) {\n let index=response.indexOf(quote); if index<0 error\n let end=index+quote.length;\n while(end<response.length && !/[.!?]/.test(response[end-1])) { end++ } // careful\n // Actually find next sentence terminator at or after quote end\n let terminal = response.slice(index+quote.length).search(/[.!?](?=\\s|$)/);\n if terminal >=0 end = index+quote.length+terminal+1;\n const sentence = response.slice(index, end).trim();\n return sentence;\n}\n```\nWhat if quote includes leading? all start at 0 or after \". \". Use indexOf. Expand end only. For H06 firs","time":{"start":1793192672648,"end":1793192681297}},"time":1793192681297}}}}
