{"record_type":"timeline-node","type":"event","timestamp":"2026-10-28T13:39:11.564Z","data":{"event":{"id":"evt_182bc7645001urFpRQCkj1Gewo","type":"message.part.updated","properties":{"sessionID":"ses_e7d7ba936ffeOnBhTlKsfisQhw","part":{"id":"prt_182bc1bdf001g6N2Jmw8t15G6K","messageID":"msg_182bc0c47001fBV9bWnlG4nNnW","sessionID":"ses_e7d7ba936ffeOnBhTlKsfisQhw","type":"reasoning","text":"Need create export script. It reads run dir (one evaluation output dir) plus dataset, emits CSVs:\n1. system-metrics.csv: extractor,baseline,goldClaims,missedClaims,spuriousClaims,accuracy, perVerdict rows maybe separate per-verdict-metrics.csv.\n2. per-claim.csv: session_id, family, claim_index, sourceQuote, benchmarkQuote?, claimType, target, gold_verdict, predicted_verdict, correct, aligned_gold_index, predicted_index, extractor, baseline, extraction_error, decision_reason, evidence_event_ids.\n3. per-verdict-metrics.csv: extractor,baseline,verdict,support,tp,fp,fn,precision,recall,f1.\n4. extraction-summary.csv: extractor,sessions,gold,predicted,matched,typeCorrect,failed,successfulEmpty.\n5. latency-summary.csv: extractor,baseline,stage,count,min,max,mean,median maybe.\n6. failures.csv.\nNeed read dataset jsonl for family mapping.\n\nPrediction items have claims array and decisions array, alignment pairs. For each prediction item (session+extractor+baseline), pair gold claims with aligned predicted. But if spurious/missed, need rows for unaligned gold and unaligned predicted. Simpler: for each gold claim index, find alignment where goldIndex===i -> predictedIndex; predicted verdict or null. For predicted claims not aligned to any gold, create row with gold null, predicted verdict. Also extraction error row? Let's do per gold row plus per spurious predicted row.\n\nFor oracle extractor, predicted = goldClaims but alignment exact; verdict from deterministic/accept/regex baselines. In predictions.jsonl, each item has extractor+baseline+claims+decisions+alignment+pairs. pairs already has expected/actual for aligned? It includes expected null for spurious and actual null for missed? Earlier for heuristic first item pairs had two rows: expected VERIFIED actual null, expected null actual VERIFIED. But for oracle baseline, pairs one row both. Good. We can use pairs to build per-claim rows. But pairs may not include sourceQuote for unaligned. Need match predicted claim by index. We'll map predicted index -> claim. For each pair:\n- expected gold claim = goldClaims[pair.goldIndex] if pair.goldIndex != null.\n- actual predicted claim = claims[pair.predictedIndex] if pair.predictedIndex != null.\nRows for each pair: session_id, family, claim_index (gold claim index or null), sourceQuote (gold or predicted), claimType (gold or predicted), target, gold_verdict, predicted_verdict (actual verdict or null), correct (expected===actual), extractor, baseline, aligned (true), pair_index.\nAlso include extraction error? If extractionError non-null, add row for session with predicted claims empty? We can add failures.csv separately.\n\nFor sessions where predicted length 0 but gold >0, pairs has expected gold actual null rows. Good.\nFor spurious predicted actual claim with gold null: row with claim_index null, sourceQuote predicted claimText, gold_verdict null, predicted_verdict actual verdict, correct null.\n\nThis per-claim CSV will be large (216 claims * 6 systems = 1296 plus spurious 0 mostly).\n\nNeed family from dataset id map. Dataset controlled-v1.jsonl has metadata.family. Good.\n\nNeed read all prediction files in run dir matching `*.jsonl`? predictions.jsonl exists. Could be one per evaluation if runner outputs one predictions.jsonl per eval. Yes output dir per evaluation contains predictions.jsonl. But export script should accept a single evaluation output dir or run dir? Let's make it accept `--run-dir` (evaluation output dir) and `--dataset`, `--out`. It will read metrics.json, predictions.jsonl, extraction.jsonl, latencies.jsonl, failures.jsonl, run-manifest.json if present. If multiple evals, user can call per eval or we can create `export-evaluation-suite.mjs` to iterate all subdirs in a suite base. Simpler: `export-evaluation-run.mjs --run-dir <eval output dir> --dataset ... --out-dir <export dir>`. Then for suite, loop subdirs in shell? Better create suite exporter that takes suite base dir and dataset, iterates immediate subdirectories that have m","time":{"start":1793194728416,"end":1793194751556}},"time":1793194751557}}}}
